WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.240
Let’s get one thing clear.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:07.680
As much as it is fun to go through the various
articles on the SCP Archive, we should take

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.280
a moment to acknowledge that it’s all fun
and games.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.920
The SCP Foundation, all the entities and objects,
creatures and people, places and stories related

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.960
to them are just that: stories.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.720
Fiction created by the authors of an online
wiki website.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.400
It’s all designed to be a bit of harmless
entertainment.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.560
But what if it isn’t harmless?

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.600
What if the characters within the world of
the SCP Archive somehow knew they had been

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.160
created by the SCP Wiki authors?

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.200
What would happen if fiction was aware that
it was, in fact, fictional?

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:49.360
Ask yourself, would you want to know if your
world, your life, everything you had ever

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.160
seen or known was all just a fantasy?

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.400
If the answer is no, then you need to stop
watching this video right now, but we’ll

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.760
warn you, just because you don’t know about
something, doesn’t necessarily stop it from

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.040
being true.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.120
Still want to find out what’s going on?

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.600
Good, then you need to hear about SCP-001.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.720
First things first, which of the various SCP-001s
are we talking about?

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.600
Anyone looking for the earliest SCP will quickly
discover that there are many different entities

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.840
and anomalies that share that number.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.960
There are a few, in-universe reasons for this.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.920
One is that all these anomalies were discovered
in the earliest days of the SCP Foundation,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.280
before their current numbering system was
introduced.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.280
Then there’s the other theories.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:41.280
For example, some have speculated that the
existence of many different SCP-001s is an

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.800
intentional misdirection by the Foundation,
their way of keeping the nature of the true

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.680
first SCP a closely-guarded secret.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:55.600
Regardless of what the reason is, the SCP-001
we’re looking at today is also known as

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.880
“The Database.”

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.680
Any and all information about The Database
is kept a secret from the entire SCP Foundation

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.760
apart from the Overseer Council.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.920
And why is it such a secret?

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.840
Well, that’s because SCP-001 contains details
of the very nature of the Foundation itself,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.520
including its creation.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.480
But we don’t mean when and how the organization
was founded, or its history.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.760
We mean how it was created, and by whom, in
our reality.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:24.720
Confused?

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.640
Well, allow us to explain it.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.760
At first, the file for SCP-001 describes a
group of humanoid entities that have an ability

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.360
to influence reality.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.480
All sounds like pretty standard, bread and
butter stuff for the SCP Foundation, right?

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.360
WRONG.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.480
These entities are, in actual fact, the authors
of the SCP Wiki themselves.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.200
As in, the people in the real world.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:53.280
The reality of the SCP Foundation is constantly
changed by these writers; written and rewritten

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.800
whenever someone edits an article on the SCP
Wiki.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.040
That’s all fine and harmless for us, but
the Wiki authors’ impact on their world

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.080
is a cause of grave concern for the Foundation.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.680
As we mentioned at the start, we all know
that the SCP Foundation and it’s vast catalogue

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.800
of user-generated articles on the creative
and the creepy are nothing more than a source

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.440
of fictional online entertainment.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.160
It can be fun to study the lore and the nuances
of the different SCPs.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.960
It can be enjoyable appreciating the creativity
and hard work that the authors put into writing

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:27.440
them.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.520
In the case of fan fiction, independent stories,
original SCPs, or even animated videos dedicated

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.880
to explaining them, it can even inspire and
fuel our own written endeavors.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.640
But, at the end of the day, when all is said
and done, we all know that the SCP Foundation

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.240
is purely fictional.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.920
What we don’t realize is that the Foundation
knows it too.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.200
Containing several pieces of information from
within their own archive, The Database contains

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.560
everything the Foundation knows about our
real world.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.320
This includes everything about the real authors
of the SCP Wiki.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.400
And you thought Deadpool was meta.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.440
You see, because the SCP Wiki has articles
from numerous different creative writers,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.640
chances are, the details of every new article
might not always line up with every previous

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:15.280
one.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.640
For a long time, there has been much discussion
amongst devoted SCP fans regarding the “canon”

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.840
of the website.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.280
Now, we don’t mean a big gun that launches
metal balls.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.400
When we say canon, we mean all of the events
and stories that are considered to be an official

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.000
part of the on-going story of the SCP Foundation.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.520
Still with us so far?

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.800
Because there are so many writers and articles
on the Wiki, the canon of the SCP Archive

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.600
is a bit inconsistent, to say the least.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.280
And that’s fine for us, because we know
none of it is real.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.560
It doesn’t have to all line up, just as
long as a good story is being told.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:58.720
But these inconsistencies; all the little
cracks of discrepancy and disparity; are what

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.760
made the Foundation themselves realize that
they are truly living in a fiction.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.920
In other words, SCP-001 is the SCP Foundation’s
window through what we would experience as

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.200
the fourth wall.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:16.080
The fourth wall is the metaphorical barrier
that separates what we know to be real, from

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.400
what we know to be purely fiction.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:23.440
In other words, it’s the line between the
audience and the fiction that they are watching,

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.120
the edge of the stage or the glass of the
television screen.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.720
And the SCP Foundation is staring through
that fourth wall, looking out at you and me.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.080
That’s right, it isn’t just the authors
that the Foundation are aware of, it’s all

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:38.960
of us.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.600
According to their own researchers, the Foundation
has determined that we, the audience and the

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.040
writers, exist in something known as the Alpha
Layer.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.800
What on Earth is an Alpha Layer?

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.960
Well, imagine our reality, the world and the
universe as we know it, is a slice of bread.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.320
It’s a strange analogy, we know, but stick
with us.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.440
Now, picture the fictional world of the SCP
Foundation as another slice of bread underneath

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.240
ours.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.560
In-between the two can be whatever you like,
but the point isn’t that the world is a

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:09.440
sandwich.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.440
The point is, that we exist on a layer of
reality above that of the SCP Wiki, and the

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.200
filling is just what keeps reality and fiction
separate.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:24.720
So, as we mentioned, the Foundation is aware
of any writers and readers of the SCP Wiki,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.960
and this does include you.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.560
But the Wiki authors are the primary focus
of The Database.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.120
After all, it’s the writers who are responsible
for creating the fictional world that the

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.360
SCP Foundation exists in.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.400
Anything that forms a part of the narrative
of that world, every article, every tale,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:47.760
every new SCP that gets published, even parts
of the Wiki that get taken down or rewritten

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.800
all have an impact on the Foundation’s universe.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.400
When authors link their new SCPs to pre-existing
ones, or create connections to a Group of

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.120
Interest like the Church of the Broken God
or the Global Occult Coalition, their writing

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.840
ends up making even more of a substantial
development to the overall narrative.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.040
And all of it ends up in The Database.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:14.720
The readers of the SCP Wiki, on the other
hand, are a smaller part of SCP-001.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.280
Mainly because they do not write or publish
the content found on the website itself.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.400
Anything that is not specifically set in stone
by a Wiki author, can be interpreted in various

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:25.680
ways by a reader.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:31.440
A reader’s interpretation is a powerful
concept, and should not be underestimated.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.000
Interpretation is the difference between reading
Animal Farm as a story about a group of animals

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.880
rebelling against their human farmer, and
understanding it as a satirical and political

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:44.160
allegory for the events leading up to the
Russian Revolution.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:48.800
So what does interpretation have to do with
SCP-001?

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.920
The point we are getting at is that any unwritten
detail or anything that isn’t explicitly

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.560
stated by a Wiki author, can be changed by
a reader’s own imagination.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.520
This can be anything, from the appearance
or personality of an SCP or character, to

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.480
a detail about the world of the Foundation.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.680
And in the same vein, if something in one
SCP article contradicts something written

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.720
in another, the reader is free to choose which
of them they accept as part of the universe’s

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:17.840
true canon.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.640
Each reader of the SCP Wiki has their own
understanding of the material written there,

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.880
and inevitably comprehends their own version
of the events within.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.960
And this is something that the SCP Foundation,
even in their world of fiction, is aware of

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:37.040
- or more specifically, the O5 Council is
aware of.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:43.920
You see, the secretive overseers are the only
ones with any knowledge of SCP-001.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.640
Only they can access The Database, only they
know the truth.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.120
The fact that they exist in this fictional
capacity is something the Overseer Council

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.160
hopes to hide from the rest of the Foundation’s
personnel.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.800
In an effort to keep the rest of their staff
from asking too many questions, the Council

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.600
has told them that there is currently no way
in existence that they could possibly contain

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.920
SCP-001.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:13.840
But, to reassure personnel, the O5s have also
stated that this does not pose any risk, nor

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:18.960
would it lead to the total and utter destruction
of their observable, fictional universe.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.080
Instead, the Council aims to focus on what
they can control, by containing any and all

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.280
information about SCP-001.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:33.280
Nothing about The Database, or the truth that
it contains, is to ever leave O5 Command under

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.440
any circumstance whatsoever.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.040
All of the information about SCP-001, or what&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.480
it contains, is kept in an&nbsp;
encrypted, highly-classified

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.800
form, at an unknown location.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.520
The key needed to decrypt this data is a pass
code split into three parts.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:54.800
This key is then memorized by members of the
O5 Council, each memorizing a different portion

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:55.920
of the pass code.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.480
It can only be accessed if every member of
the Council unanimously agrees that it is

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.240
absolutely necessary.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.760
And even when they do decrypt the information
about SCP-001, it remains for the eyes of

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.920
O5 Command, and strictly their eyes only.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:18.240
Any member of the SCP Foundation caught leaking
data about SCP-001 is to be contained by any

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.680
means necessary.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.840
This also includes if a member of personnel
attempts to get this classified information

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.640
out via espionage, psychic leakage, or other
devious methods.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.960
Foundation researchers are even banned from
conducting any of their own investigations

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:38.400
into the nature of SCP-001, or the data it
contains.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.440
If a lower level staff member was to somehow
find out the truth, that the entire Foundation

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:47.920
is just a fiction, then that person would
be at the mercy of the most senior figure

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.600
on the O5 Council.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:53.760
It would be up to that senior figure to decide
the staff member’s fate.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:57.840
Anyone with high enough clearance or status
within the Foundation who found out about

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.640
SCP-001, would alternatively be given a Class
A amnestic.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.360
These are memory altering drugs that the SCP
Foundation uses to keep information about

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.040
anomalies a secret.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.680
Apparently, even from its own staff.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:16.320
Still, having your mind wiped has got to be
better than being killed or contained, just

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.080
for realizing you’re a fictional character
on an online wiki.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:25.440
The O5 Council have plans in mind for us,
and for the SCP Wiki writers responsible for

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:26.400
creating them.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.920
The SCP Wiki entry on The Database closes
with a proposal; a way that the O5 Council

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:36.960
can reach out of their fiction, directly to
the wiki’s authors in the real world.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:37.840
From there?

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.640
Their goal is simple: through memetic kill
agents, the SCP Foundation will put the Wiki

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:44.800
authors to sleep and then kill them.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.800
Sounds pretty far-fetched, doesn’t it?

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:52.000
After all, when was the last time something
fictional killed someone in our real world?

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.680
Even more bizarre, what if this kill agent
already exists?

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:00.160
The O5 Council must have known for a while
that their world is fictional, so they’ve

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:05.600
had plenty of time to develop a kill agent,
some way of reaching us and the SCP Wiki authors

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.040
in the real world.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:13.760
Worse still, what if this kill agent is already
out there somewhere, lurking on the SCP Archive,

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:16.320
just waiting for you to find it?

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.040
And if you read it, how would it kill you?

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:22.080
If you don’t want to know, then that’s
your choice to make.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:30.240
But if curiosity is getting the better of
you, then remember this: Beware SCP-5999.

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:36.880
To continue the story, go check out “The
Last SCP - SCP-5999 - This is Where I Died”,

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:43.360
and for more information on SCP-001, go check
out “SCP-001 - Which is the Real 001?”

